<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rim-Sin I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1822–1763 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a> · Also known as Rim-Sin, the man of Larsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rim-Sin I was a long-reigning king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and one of the most powerful rulers of the region in the early second millennium BCE. A contemporary letter quoted in accounts of the Babylonian Empire name him alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> as one of several kings of comparable strength. Hammurabi eventually defeated Larsa, removing Rim-Sin I and bringing the south under Babylonian control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i.jpg" alt="Rim-Sin I" width="235" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rim-Sin_foundation_figurine,_1822-1763_BC_-_Oriental_Institute_Museum,_University_of_Chicago_-_DSC07168.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…from the archive of Mari captures Babylon's position with laconic precision: “No king is powerful by himself: with Hammurabi, the man of Babylon, go 10 or 15 kings; so with <b>Rim-Sin, the man of Larsa</b>” — Babylon was simply one of several comparable kingdoms, neither dominant nor subordinate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They allow the reigns of Shamshi-Adad, Hammurabi, <b>Rim-Sin of Larsa</b> and Yarim-Lim of Yamhad to be followed almost month by month, and they record the international system of the Middle Bronze Age, in which kings addressed each other as father, son or brother according to their rank.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…period, when the occupied area reached 140 hectares, the merchant Ea-nasir dealt in copper from Dilmun, shipping ingots that originated in Magan by water to Ur for resale and probably supplying the palace; he was active in the 11th and 19th regnal years of <b>Rim-Sin I</b> of Larsa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its last military triumph came when Utu-hengal defeated the Gutian king Tirigan, c. 2119 BCE, and the city fell to <b>Rim-Sin of Larsa</b>, c. 1802 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1794 BCE <b>Rim-Sin I</b> of Larsa conquered it.</blockquote><a href="/state/isin">Isin</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1834 BCE an Amorite leader from Emutbal with an Elamite name, Kudur-Mabuk, installed his sons Warad-Sin and then <b>Rim-Sin I</b> as kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/larsa">Larsa</a></li></ul></section>
